The file is:

**`gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/internal/bind/EnumTypeAdapter.java`**

Specifically, the `nameToConstant` map is built in the private constructor `EnumTypeAdapter(Class<T> classOfT)` at lines 89–105. That's where the bug lives:

```java
for (Field constantField : fields) {
  ...
  String name = constant.name();
  ...
  SerializedName annotation = constantField.getAnnotation(SerializedName.class);
  if (annotation != null) {
    name = annotation.value();
    for (String alternate : annotation.alternate()) {
      nameToConstant.put(alternate, constant);
    }
  }
  nameToConstant.put(name, constant);
  ...
}
```

At a glance this looks right — alternates are added via `annotation.alternate()`. The actual failure mode to check for is a **later enum constant silently overwriting** an earlier one's alternate-name entry when two constants share an alternate/name string (last-write-wins via plain `HashMap.put`), or — more likely given the bug report — verify how `alternate()`'s array is populated/iterated when `SerializedName` has only `alternate` values and no distinct primary name change edge cases. Want me to dig into `read()` (lines 113+) as well to see how `nameToConstant` is consulted during deserialization, to pin down exactly why an alternate name fails to resolve?
